===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|Americano}}

===Etymology===
1970s, American {{der|en|es|americano||American (coffee)}}. Cocktail sense from {{der|en|it|americano}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A drink made of [[espresso]] [[coffee]] diluted with hot water.
#: {{syn|en|cafe americano|caffè americano|coffee americano}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Ronald H. Balson|title=The Trust|publisher=St. Martin's Press|isbn=9781250127464|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-dM4DgAAQBAJ&lpg=PA16&dq=cup%20of%20%22americano%22&hl=es&pg=PA16#v=onepage&q&f=false|text=“No, the coffee. Do you want a cup of '''Americano'''?” I groaned. Welcome to Europe. “Can't I just get a cup of regular coffee? A little cream, a little sugar?” {{...|She closed her eyes and exhaled through her nose. She was dealing with an idiot.}} “An '''Americano''' is a shot of espresso with hot water added.” “I don't know why they call it '''Americano'''. No one in America drinks espresso and hot water.”}}
# A [[cocktail]] made with [[Campari]], sweet [[vermouth]]{{,}} and [[club soda]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Eric Felten|title=How's Your Drink?: Cocktails, Culture, and the Art of Drinking Well|year=2009|publisher=Agate Publishing|pages=37–38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ANSXqicDb4IC&lpg=PA38&dq=drink%20%22americano%22&hl=es&pg=PA37#v=onepage&q&f=false|isbn=978-1-57284-612-8|passage=An '''Americano''' is made of Campari, sweet vermouth, and soda water over ice in a highball glass. {{...}} The drink was so popular among Americans visiting Italy at the turn of the last century that it was named after them.}}

